 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1050 1200.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b8ced6b0-8e1a-57eb-9f2f-b4bd8eace852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180029 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180029 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180029 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180029 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d6daea5f-9d1c-5bf1-b6af-12c3102e71d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180029 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-x=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180504 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180504 #edb/ref 1061 35826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180504 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4fded59b-3b34-5c3a-b5bc-fbdf60f1028f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180504 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180505 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180505 #edb/ref 1061 35827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180505 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a52a1b21-2c56-5112-aa12-6e43086599bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180505 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180506 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180506 #edb/ref 1061 35829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180506 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb889341-22d5-52e6-9979-1fd814bc5cec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180506 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180507 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180507 #edb/ref 1061 35863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180507 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9195b98-bf60-5d80-a9f1-4dbe4c7ec614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180507 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180509 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180509 #edb/ref 1061 35901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180509 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6cc7967-4281-527a-8021-7768a792728f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180509 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180510 #edb/ref 1061 35902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180510 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7543ee2e-ca36-5209-8a6f-1fae02154a3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180510 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180725 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ and $C$ be a *closed* curve in the $xy$-plane. Recall that the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nCalculating flux using this definition is often cumbersome because we need to calculate $\\mathbf n,$ the outward-pointing unit normal vector. \n\nIn practice, we often use the following alternative formula to calculate the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson.\n\n**Watch Out!** When applying this formula, we must choose a positively-oriented parameterization for $C.$ This formula will *not* work if $C$ is negatively oriented!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180725 #edb/ref 1065 5851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180725 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c74e835b-f210-5a77-8d6e-aef474c89522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180725 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180727 #edb/ref 1064 "As we've seen, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ through a piecewise-smooth, closed curve $C$ is a unique scalar number that represents the net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$ \n\nThe question now is, does the notion of flux exist for curves that are not closed?\n\nIndeed, it is possible to define the notion of flux through a non-closed curve. However, there is one crucial difference we need to bear in mind:\n\n- In the case of a smooth, closed curve, it is always clear which direction is \"out.\" Therefore, the outward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ is unique at every point on $C.$\n\n- However, on a smooth, non-closed curve, there is no clear notion of \"in\" and \"out.\" Instead, we can choose between *two* normal vectors at any point on the curve, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d1/d193eb8109ead15de50c06a7bb0b654243173d882bd361f9fd34026aa23c1b5f.png)\n\nTherefore, when computing the flux of a vector field through a non-closed curve, we first specify the direction that the unit normal points and then measure the flux relative to that direction. \n\nFor example, for the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and curve $C$ below, one choice for the unit normal will give a positive number, and the other a negative number.\n\n![](images/4f/4f92dd5334b5836703857c081049f126d39c141e84d8e94171e338a162cf3cc4.png)\n\nFinally, to calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across a non-closed curve $C,$ we can continue to use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** To apply this formula, we usually need to parameterize $C.$ It is *vital* that we pick a parameterization where the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ is a $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180727 #edb/ref 1065 5869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180727 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e2794e26-620d-59e9-85de-a3bb49f6991a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180727 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating Flux for Non-Closed Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180728 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now derive the following formula for the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that if $C$ is parameterized by the function $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ then \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{y'(t)\\,\\mathbf i - x'(t)\\,\\mathbf j}{||\\mathbf r'(t)||},\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that $C$ is positively-oriented. \n\nSubstituting our expression for $\\mathbf n$ into our line integral definition of flux and using the fact that $\\textrm d s = ||\\mathbf r'(t)|| \\,\\textrm d t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||})||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\cancel{||{r}^{′}(t)||}})\\cancel{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot ({y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P(x(t),y(t)){y}^{′}(t)-Q(x(t),y(t)){x}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180728 #edb/ref 1065 5870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180728 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cc71a06d-4832-5949-91b4-71cb0f8185ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180728 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Main Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260290 #edb/ref 1080 "54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260290 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260290 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e1109ed-b141-519b-a9c0-7e14a71dad93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260308 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{9}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260308 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260308 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2fd158a1-8e1c-5409-9f7d-cc713afefd91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260512 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{9}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260512 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260512 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "49e0677d-703c-5fd2-bba6-5d10e8428357" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260522 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260522 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260522 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b4f1bb5-5af8-54dc-b770-fd3f05a8066c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260678 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260678 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260678 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d592e34-b162-5fde-8afc-943efe3e8ed5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260788 #edb/ref 1080 "-14\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260788 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260788 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6893a418-d322-5ab1-83ac-21e6380ffd8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260835 #edb/ref 1080 "25\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260835 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260835 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6d6e8dc0-de0b-5246-baf1-2e88a94ead89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261048 #edb/ref 1080 "-75\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261048 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261048 #edb/r